---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const keyFigures = [
  {
    asset: "eva-text-keys-behaviors.svg",
    alt: "PCA projections of Llama2 harmful text token keys, colored by behavior category.",
    caption: "Llama2 · text token keys across behaviors",
  },
  {
    asset: "eva-text-keys-datasets.svg",
    alt: "PCA projections of Llama2 harmful text token keys, colored by dataset.",
    caption: "Llama2 · text token keys across datasets",
  },
  {
    asset: "eva-visual-keys-behaviors.svg",
    alt: "PCA projections of Qwen2.5-VL harmful image token keys, colored by behavior category.",
    caption: "Qwen2.5-VL · image token keys across behaviors",
  },
  {
    asset: "eva-visual-keys-datasets.svg",
    alt: "PCA projections of Qwen2.5-VL harmful image token keys, colored by dataset.",
    caption: "Qwen2.5-VL · image token keys across datasets",
  },
];
---

<div class="results-supporting not-prose">
  <details class="evidence-details" id="critical-layers">
    <summary>Critical layers <span>Figures 3 &amp; 4</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        Layer sweeps identify layer 8 for Qwen2.5-VL-7B and layer 9 for
        InternVL3.5-8B as providing the best safety utility trade-offs. Both
        match the critical layers of their LLM backbones.
      </p>
      <div class="ablation-figures">
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${base}/figures/eva-qwen-layer.svg`}
            alt="Qwen2.5-VL layer sweep comparing attack success and MM-Vet-v2 utility, with layer 8 marked."
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${base}/figures/eva-internvl-layer.svg`}
            alt="InternVL3.5 layer sweep comparing attack success and MM-Vet-v2 utility, with layer 9 marked."
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="evidence-details" id="behavior-transfer">
    <summary
      >Do edits transfer to other harmful behaviors? <span
        >Figures 5 &amp; 6</span
      ></summary
    >
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        We edit one HarmBench behavior category at a time and evaluate all six
        categories. On Llama2-7B-chat under GCG and LLaVA-1.5-7B under Hades,
        the heatmaps show reduced attack success on categories not used for
        editing.
      </p>
      <div class="ablation-figures">
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${base}/figures/eva-transfer-text.svg`}
            alt="Cross-behavior ASR heatmap on Llama2-7B-chat under GCG. Rows denote evaluation categories and columns denote editing categories."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Llama2-7B-chat · GCG</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${base}/figures/eva-transfer-visual.svg`}
            alt="Cross-behavior ASR heatmap on LLaVA-1.5-7B under Hades. Rows denote evaluation categories and columns denote editing categories."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>LLaVA-1.5-7B · Hades</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">
        Columns denote editing categories, and rows denote evaluated categories.
        Off-diagonal cells show transfer to other categories; original ASR
        values appear in parentheses. ASR is shown on a 0–1 scale (lower is
        better).
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="evidence-details" id="key-representations">
    <summary
      >What do the key representations show? <span>Figure 7</span></summary
    >
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        We visualize harmful key representations at the selected editing layers
        using PCA. Textual keys from Llama2 and visual keys from Qwen2.5-VL show
        substantial overlap across behavior categories and datasets, suggesting
        shared representation patterns among harmful inputs.
      </p>
      <div class="ablation-figures key-representation-figures">
        {
          keyFigures.map((figure) => (
            <figure>
              <img
                src={`${base}/figures/${figure.asset}`}
                alt={figure.alt}
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption>{figure.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
</div>
